body:
Recap of open questions and proposals (as of May 27): 

1. **Controller pattern for custom elements.** 
Per @annevk's and @smaug----'s [feedback](https://github.com/whatwg/dom/pull/1404#discussion_r3281660541), have an AbortController-style `OpaqueRangeController` for custom elements, while native form controls keep `createValueRange(start, end)` (e.g. `input.createValueRange(start, end)`).

Example proposed IDLs:

```webidl
interface OpaqueRange : AbstractRange {
  DOMRectList getClientRects();
  DOMRect    getBoundingClientRect();
  undefined disconnect(); // do we still want disconnect() here, on the controller, or neither?
};

interface OpaqueRangeController {
  constructor(Element element);
  readonly attribute OpaqueRange range;
  undefined setOffsets(unsigned long start, unsigned long end);
};   
```

Example of a custom element using it:

```js
class ImageGallery extends HTMLElement {
  #controller = new OpaqueRangeController(this);

  constructor() {
    super();
    const shadow = this.attachShadow({ mode: "closed" });

    shadow.innerHTML = `
      <div class="frame"><img src="dog.jpg"></div>
      <div class="frame"><img src="cat.jpg"></div>
      <div class="frame"><img src="cow.jpg"></div>
    `;
  }

  // Read-only range over the gallery's images.
  // Offset units are defined by the element; here they're image indices.
  get selectionRange() {
    return this.#controller.range;
  }

  select(startIndex, endIndex) {
    this.#controller.setOffsets(startIndex, endIndex);
  }
}
customElements.define("image-gallery", ImageGallery);
```

```html
<image-gallery></image-gallery>
```

```js
const gallery = document.querySelector("image-gallery");
gallery.select(1, 2);
```
A regular Range can't reach into the closed shadow DOM where the images live, so the gallery vends an OpaqueRange instead.

Would appreciate input on:
   - Geometry for custom-element ranges: well-defined for form controls (UA walks the inner editor); no equivalent for custom elements. Options: controller takes a geometry callback, or geometry returns null/empty.
   - How the custom element signals content length changes to the controller: native form controls get auto-clamping for free when value changes; custom elements don't. Need a way for the element to say "my content shrank, please clamp": either via a length-setting method on the controller, a generic notify, or making it the element's responsibility.
   - Whether `disconnect()` belongs on the controller only, or also on the range, or neither.


2. **Offsets question + @LeaVerou's proposal in [whatwg/html#12475](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/12475).** 
Lea proposes `textStart`/`textEnd` on `AbstractRange` as character offsets with one consistent meaning. For OpaqueRange, this solves the "what do `startOffset`/`endOffset` mean here" debate and maps cleanly to what the Microsoft Editor SDK already uses offsets for (`value.substring(range.startOffset, range.endOffset)`). Want to gauge interest and address the open questions she raised, e.g. what the offsets are measured from for a generic Range (`commonAncestorContainer`, the root, etc).

3. **What stays on `OpaqueRange` itself.** 
Do we keep inherited `startOffset`/`endOffset`, drop them in favor of `textStart`/`textEnd`, [add a `valueFromRange()` to `input` and `textarea`](https://github.com/whatwg/dom/pull/1404#discussion_r3272251631), etc. Tied to (2).

I really want to emphasise this again:

> I'll stress: ultimately all of this design is not something out of the realms of custom element authors today, so there is less burden on us to provide a satisfactory interface just for this; yes it is nice, but really `OpaqueRangeController`/`OpaqueRange` for custom element authors is little more than `WeakSet`/`{ startOffset, endOffset }`.

I'm happy to have this work for Custom Elements but we really should avoid muddying the design for something which is already ergonomically simple enough for a custom element.

It's not really difficult for a custom element to vend exactly the kind of opaque objects they want, for example:

```js
const pass = Symbol();
class OpaqueSelection {
  #rectFor;
  constructor(key = null, rectFor = null) {
    if (pass !== key || !rectFor) throw new TypeError("Illegal constructor");
    this.#rectFor = rectFor;
  }

  getBoundingClientRect() {
    return this.#rectFor();
  }
}

class ImageGallery extends HTMLElement {
  #root = this.attachShadow({ mode: "closed" });

  select(a, b) {
    return new OpaqueSelection(pass, () => {
      const imgs = [...this.#root.querySelectorAll("img")];
      const [start, end] = [Math.min(a, b), Math.max(a, b)];
      const rects = imgs.slice(start, end + 1).map(img => img.getBoundingClientRect());
      return union(rects);
    });
  }
}

function union(rects) {
  if (!rects.length) return new DOMRect();

  const left = Math.min(...rects.map(r => r.left));
  const top = Math.min(...rects.map(r => r.top));
  const right = Math.max(...rects.map(r => r.right));
  const bottom = Math.max(...rects.map(r => r.bottom));

  return new DOMRect(left, top, right - left, bottom - top);
}
```

So we have to ask ourselves if we're contorting this API to save a dozen LOC for Custom Element authors, while potentially hampering the use cases for Input/TextArea, is that an efficient tradeoff?

Start and end integers _do_ make sense for textarea & input. If they make sense for custom elements then great, if not, then Custom Element authors can use a variety of objects including their own for those other purposes.

Hi all, thank you for discussing this at WEH. Since I was OOO, I've gone over the [notes](https://hackmd.io/rAkZL3HnQmiwyMoFBCpGjA#OpaqueRange), but part of the transcription seems incomplete, so I wanted to check my understanding.


For custom elements, I’m not sure whether folks had in mind an explicit controller object or a revealing-constructor/private-updater pattern. Here's a rough sketch:

### Option A: explicit controller
```webidl
interface OpaqueRange : AbstractRange {
  DOMRectList getClientRects();
  DOMRect getBoundingClientRect();
};

interface OpaqueRangeController {
  constructor(Element host);
  readonly attribute OpaqueRange range;

  undefined setRange(
    Node startNode,
    unsigned long startOffset,
    unsigned long exposedStartOffset, // fake start offset; name tbd
    Node endNode,
    unsigned long endOffset,
    unsigned long exposedEndOffset // fake end offset; name tbd
  );
};
```

### Option B: no controller interface; constructor has a private updater
```webidl
dictionary OpaqueRangeUpdate {
  required Node startNode;
  required unsigned long startOffset;
  required Node endNode;
  required unsigned long endOffset;

  unsigned long exposedStartOffset; // fake start offset
  unsigned long exposedEndOffset; // fake end offset
};

callback OpaqueRangeUpdater = undefined (OpaqueRangeUpdate update);
callback OpaqueRangeInitializer = undefined (OpaqueRangeUpdater updater);

interface OpaqueRange : AbstractRange {
  constructor(Element host, OpaqueRangeInitializer initializer);
  DOMRectList getClientRects();
  DOMRect getBoundingClientRect();
};
```

For both options, the node/offset pairs are the hidden boundary points browser APIs would use, while the exposed offsets are placeholders for any public/fake endpoints we decide to expose.

From the discussion, it seems that cross-shadow-boundary selection exposure and canvas-backed/arbitrary JS-backed range geometry/painting were left out of scope for now.

Does this sound right?

My general idea was to delegate the API to init members, whenever they were queried. So e.g.:

```js
new OpaqueRange({
  getBoundingClientRect() { return new DOMRect(...) }
  get startOffset() { return ... }
})
```

The idea being that the OpaqueRange machinery would consult the init dictionary, and if a member was present, that code would be evaluated instead of the normal logic. This way vendors of Opaque Ranges could selectively override parts, as they see fit.
